<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Shabankara</h1><p class="st-meta">1030 CE – 1355 CE (approx.) · capital Ij</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Kurdish tribal confederation of eastern Fars that overthrew the last Buyids of Shiraz and ruled from the fortress of Ij for three centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Shabankara were Kurdish pastoral tribes in the uplands of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a>, around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darab">Darabgerd</a>. In 1062 their chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fadluya">Fadluya</a> (Fadlawayh) seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a> from the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyid</a> ruler. He soon came into conflict with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire</a>, which defeated him, and he died a prisoner around 1071.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fadluya">Fadluya</a> the Shabankara chiefs held the region of Shabankara with its capital at the fortress of Ij, near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estahban">Estahban</a>, ruling as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/salghurid-dynasty">Salghurid dynasty</a> and later the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a>. Their society combined pastoral nomadism with control of irrigated farmland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravan-trade">caravan routes</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> they paid tribute while keeping their tribal leadership.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the 14th century the Shabankara were weakened by Ilkhanid succession wars. In 1355 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/muzaffarid-dynasty">Muzaffarid dynasty</a> took Ij and ended their rule.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/shabankara.jpg" alt="Shabankara" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: Gueevkobani · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shabankara.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1030 CE – 1355 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ij</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Fadluya</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kurdish, Persian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Shabankareh, Shabankara'i</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1192&amp;focus=shabankara" aria-label="Shabankara on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6096 1116 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Shabankara lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6416 1372L6481 1127M6416 1372L6134 1224M6416 1372L6367 1352"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6481 1127h0M6134 1224h0M6367 1352h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6416 1372h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6416 1372h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Ij. <a href="/atlas?year=1192&amp;focus=shabankara">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

